<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monnet Plan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Monnet Plan was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first plan for reconstruction and modernisation, drawn up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-monnet">Jean Monnet</a>'s planning commission and adopted in 1946. It directed investment to basic industries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, electricity, cement, farm machinery and transport, and was partly financed by Marshall aid. It set the model of indicative planning that guided French economic growth in the following decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monnet-plan.jpg" alt="Monnet Plan" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (Keystone France) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Monnet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> came out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> badly weakened. Its economy had stagnated even before the war, and by 1945 national income in real terms was little more than half that of 1929. Productivity per worker was a third of the U.S. level, chiefly because of low investment: France had one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractor</a> for every 200 people on the land, the U.S. one for every 43. The plan, officially the Modernization and Re-equipment Plan, set out to raise investment and to change habits of thought along with machinery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, as chairman of the Provisional Government, set up the General Planning Commission on 3 January 1946, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-monnet">Jean Monnet</a> at its head. Monnet insisted that it be attached to the prime minister's office rather than an economic ministry. Its staff never exceeded thirty-five senior officials, but more than a thousand people from firms, unions and the civil service served on its eighteen Modernization Commissions. The plan set targets for 1950 in six basic sectors: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, electricity, rail transport, cement and farm machinery. It had no means of enforcement, but coal, electricity and rail were already nationalised and steel received state capital on condition of mergers.</p><p>In March 1946 a French delegation went to Washington, and on 28 May an agreement brought about $1 billion of new funding and the prospect of a $500 million loan from the World Bank, less than the $3 billion requested. The plan went to the government on 27 November 1946. After the harsh year of 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> began in April 1948, and the Monnet Plan was extended to 1952 to match it. Just under $3 billion of French aid went to the plan's aims, through counterpart funds that supplied between 50 and 90 percent of its resources in 1949 and 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Not every target was met, but Yergin and Stanislaw credit the plan with giving discipline and direction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at a crucial time, and it set the country on the road to its economic growth of the 1950s. Its expected reliance on German coal led to the Schuman Plan of 9 May 1950 and so to the European Coal and Steel Community. The Planning Commission went on producing multi-year plans until 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monnet Plan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It nationalised Renault, coal, electricity and major banks, created the social security system and the <b>Monnet Plan</b> for reconstruction, and oversaw the trials of collaborators.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">Provisional Government of the French Republic</a></li></ul></section>
